\begin{abstract}
In this paper, we investigate the asymptotic spectral properties of $n \times n$ banded Toeplitz matrices as $n \to \infty$. Recalling the classical theorems of Schmidt--Spitzer and Hirschman, which describe the accumulation of eigenvalues along a set of curves in the complex plane, we re-examine this limiting behavior through the lens of modern potential theory. We frame the limiting eigenvalue distribution as the first component of a vector measure that uniquely minimizes a logarithmic energy functional within a vector equilibrium problem (VEP). Furthermore, we show that the remaining components of the equilibrium vector measure, along with their associated supports and external fields, are directly related to the generalized eigenvalues of the operator's symbol.
\end{abstract}

